1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,787
And then, in the third week,

2
00:00:02,013 --> 00:00:03,838
the house gave up its first secret.

3
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:05,163
In the master study,

4
00:00:05,450 --> 00:00:07,625
the crew was pulling up warped oak planks

5
00:00:07,638 --> 00:00:09,762
when the crowbar struck something that wasn't wood

6
00:00:09,888 --> 00:00:10,462
or stone.

7
00:00:10,875 --> 00:00:11,638
It was the dull,

8
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:12,962
hollow thud of metal.

9
00:00:13,388 --> 00:00:15,800
Wedged between two load-bearing joists,

10
00:00:16,062 --> 00:00:17,750
buried in a century of dust,

11
00:00:18,138 --> 00:00:19,838
sat a heavy copper lockbox,

12
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:21,250
hidden there on purpose.

13
00:00:21,662 --> 00:00:23,162
Inside, there was no gold.

14
00:00:23,575 --> 00:00:24,287
No jewels.

15
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:26,625
Just a stack of brittle letters wrapped in

16
00:00:26,638 --> 00:00:29,050
oilcloth, the ink faded to sepia.

17
00:00:29,475 --> 00:00:31,462
They were written by the original architect,

18
00:00:31,688 --> 00:00:35,250
three months before the mansion's grand opening in 1912.

19
00:00:35,662 --> 00:00:37,262
And they were not letters of triumph.

20
00:00:37,688 --> 00:00:39,088
They were letters of desperation.

21
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:41,787
The architect was writing to a silent partner

22
00:00:41,888 --> 00:00:43,850
about a catastrophic shortage of money.

23
00:00:44,262 --> 00:00:45,812
To finish the grand facade,

24
00:00:46,175 --> 00:00:47,775
corners had to be cut where no one

25
00:00:47,788 --> 00:00:48,338
could see them.

26
00:00:48,762 --> 00:00:51,363
But beneath the letters was something even stranger.

27
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:52,475
Hold that thought.

28
00:00:52,888 --> 00:00:53,675
We'll come back to it.
